## Licensing history

Three distinct licensing statements exist upstream. They are set out
separately below, because collapsing them into a single narrative is what
produced the error corrected at the end of this section.

1.  @plamzi's MUDPortal-Web-App repository (created 2013) carries the full
    text of the GNU General Public License, **version 2**, at its root.

2.  Within that repository, the single file this project descends from,
    `proxy/wsproxy.js`, carries its own header reading `MIT license`. A
    repository-level license does not displace a file's own designation, so
    the terms attaching to the code inherited here are MIT. Item 1 is
    context; it is not a constraint on this file.

3.  In 2020, @neverbot's `maldorne/mud-web-proxy` added a `LICENSE.md`
    (commit `2b1acf7`, "feat: license and readme files") stating GNU GPL
    **version 3 or later** and naming both copyright holders. That file is
    headed `mud-web-client` — the name of the sibling client project, not
    the proxy. The mislabeling propagated into this repository and is among
